1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Session eight.

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,900
Session seven asked what a model can represent.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:11,725
One weighted sum draws one straight boundary; add a hidden layer and you can draw more than one.

4
00:00:11,725 --> 00:00:14,675
That was a question about capability.

5
00:00:14,675 --> 00:00:21,225
Today's question is different, and in practice it is the one that decides whether your work is worth anything.

6
00:00:21,225 --> 00:00:24,475
You have trained a model and it produces numbers.

7
00:00:24,475 --> 00:00:27,325
How do you know it learned anything useful?

8
00:00:27,325 --> 00:00:30,800
How do you know it will work on data it has never seen?

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:33,450
And how would you know if it had not?

10
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:36,800
I want to be blunt about why this session exists.

11
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:46,675
Almost every serious mistake I see in this field, in student projects and in published papers, is a mistake of evaluation rather than a mistake of modelling.

12
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:48,675
The model is fine.

13
00:00:48,675 --> 00:00:58,000
The number reported for it is wrong, usually in the optimistic direction, and usually for a reason that is completely invisible unless you know to look for it.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,450
So today has three blocks.

15
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:08,100
First, how to pose a problem: supervised and unsupervised, regression and classification, and what follows from that choice.

16
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:18,400
Second, and this is the heart of it, how we know a model learned anything: three data sets, the metrics, and the ways that information leaks from your test set into your training.

17
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:25,025
Third, the practical move to PyTorch, so that from Session nine you can build and train networks properly.

18
00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:28,600
By the end you should be suspicious in a useful way.

19
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:30,625
That is the skill.

20
00:01:30,625 --> 00:01:36,550
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session eight page of the course book.

21
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:44,775
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

22
00:01:44,775 --> 00:01:48,800
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

23
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:55,075
Let me start with a demonstration, because it makes the point faster than any definition.

24
00:01:55,075 --> 00:01:58,775
On this slide is one model, evaluated twice.

25
00:01:58,775 --> 00:02:04,125
The model is one-nearest-neighbour, which is about the simplest thing you can call a model.

26
00:02:04,125 --> 00:02:12,850
To predict the helix fraction of a protein, it looks through the training proteins, finds the one most similar to yours, and gives you that one's answer.

27
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:16,050
There is nothing to fit; it just remembers.

28
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:19,950
The middle panel evaluates it on the data it was built from.

29
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:23,300
Every point sits exactly on the diagonal.

30
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:26,375
R squared is one point zero zero.

31
00:02:26,375 --> 00:02:27,575
Perfect.

32
00:02:27,575 --> 00:02:34,975
Now, before you look at the right panel, predict: how well will this perfect model do on fourteen proteins it has never seen?

33
00:02:34,975 --> 00:02:38,725
The right panel: R squared of nought point zero three.

34
00:02:38,725 --> 00:02:45,925
Which, as we will discuss later, is essentially the same as having no model at all and always predicting the average.

35
00:02:45,925 --> 00:02:48,650
And notice there is no bug here.

36
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:51,400
The middle panel is not a mistake.

37
00:02:51,400 --> 00:03:04,775
One-nearest-neighbour genuinely does get every training protein right, for a completely uninteresting reason: when you look up the protein most similar to protein X and protein X is in the collection, you find protein X.

38
00:03:04,775 --> 00:03:09,650
It finds itself, with perfect similarity, and returns its own answer.

39
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:12,675
That is the whole session in one slide.

40
00:03:12,675 --> 00:03:18,500
The same model, the same code, two evaluations, and one of the two numbers is meaningless.

41
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:23,375
Everything we do today is machinery for making sure you report the right one.

42
00:03:23,975 --> 00:03:25,450
Block one.

43
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,350
Posing the problem.

44
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:31,200
On the page, this block is the section "Posing the problem".

45
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:43,450
Before you can evaluate a model you have to decide what you are asking it to do, and that decision has consequences all the way down: which loss you use, which metrics mean anything, and how hard the problem is.

46
00:03:43,450 --> 00:03:48,100
This block is about making that decision deliberately rather than by habit.

47
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:54,300
Two words to get out of the way first, because they divide up everything in machine learning.

48
00:03:54,300 --> 00:04:00,900
Supervised learning means you have labelled examples: inputs where somebody already knows the right answer.

49
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:07,700
You fit the model so that its outputs match those known answers, and then you use it on inputs where nobody knows.

50
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:12,450
Session seven's perceptron fitted to known binding sites is supervised.

51
00:04:12,450 --> 00:04:16,125
Almost everything we train in this course is supervised.

52
00:04:16,125 --> 00:04:19,875
Unsupervised learning means you have no labels at all.

53
00:04:19,875 --> 00:04:25,100
You are not fitting to known answers; you are looking for structure in the data itself.

54
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:34,450
Clustering sequences into families is the example you will see today: nobody tells the algorithm which family is which, it groups them by similarity.

55
00:04:34,450 --> 00:04:39,800
The MMseqs2 clustering we use later in this session is unsupervised.

56
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:45,875
And there is an interesting case in between, which is worth noticing because you have already built one.

57
00:04:45,875 --> 00:04:51,000
A position-specific scoring matrix from Session five is not quite either.

58
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,350
It is counted from a set of family members, not fitted to labelled positive and negative examples.

59
00:04:57,350 --> 00:05:00,625
Nobody gave it negatives to push away from.

60
00:05:00,625 --> 00:05:06,800
So it is not supervised in the sense we mean today, but it is not looking for unknown structure either.

61
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:14,550
Keep the distinction in mind mostly for one reason: the evaluation machinery in Block two is built for supervised learning.

62
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:21,825
Evaluating an unsupervised method is a harder and murkier business, because there is no right answer to compare with.

63
00:05:22,425 --> 00:05:29,075
Within supervised learning there is a second fork, and it is decided entirely by what your label is.

64
00:05:29,075 --> 00:05:32,550
If the label is a number, you are doing regression.

65
00:05:32,550 --> 00:05:36,375
The model outputs a number, and you ask how close it is.

66
00:05:36,375 --> 00:05:42,600
Examples from our field: the helix fraction of a protein, which is a number between nought and one.

67
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:45,600
The melting temperature of a protein.

68
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:51,400
The change in stability when you mutate a residue, which people write as delta delta G.

69
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,425
An expression level.

70
00:05:53,425 --> 00:05:55,525
A binding affinity.

71
00:05:55,525 --> 00:05:59,525
If the label is a category, you are doing classification.

72
00:05:59,525 --> 00:06:05,500
The model outputs a class, or a probability for each class, and you ask how often it is right.

73
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:09,550
Examples: which compartment of the cell a protein goes to.

74
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:12,750
Is this variant pathogenic or benign.

75
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:15,950
Does this protein have a signal peptide.

76
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:21,700
And the per-residue one from Session seven: is this residue helix, strand or coil.

77
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:34,250
This is not a deep distinction, but it is a consequential one, because everything downstream follows from it: which loss function you can use, which metrics are meaningful, and even which model architectures make sense.

78
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:37,250
We will come back to that in a few slides.

79
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:44,650
And here is the thing that is worth pausing on: the same biology can usually be posed either way, and you choose.

80
00:06:44,650 --> 00:06:47,100
Take protein stability.

81
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:53,825
You can ask the regression question: by how many kilojoules per mole does this mutation change the stability?

82
00:06:53,825 --> 00:07:01,175
Or you can put a threshold on it and ask the classification question: is this mutation destabilising, yes or no?

83
00:07:01,175 --> 00:07:03,500
Take our helix fraction.

84
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:07,200
Regression: what fraction of this protein is helical?

85
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:11,425
Classification: is this protein mostly helical or not?

86
00:07:11,425 --> 00:07:18,650
Putting a threshold on a number turns regression into classification, and it almost always makes the problem easier.

87
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:23,400
The model no longer has to get the value right, only the side of the line.

88
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:29,475
If your data is noisy, or your labels are uncertain, that can be a genuinely good decision.

89
00:07:29,475 --> 00:07:32,375
But be clear about what you paid for it.

90
00:07:32,375 --> 00:07:35,600
You have thrown information away, twice over.

91
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:51,275
You threw it away in the labels: a protein at nought point four nine and a protein at nought point zero one are now both in the same class, and a protein at nought point four nine and one at nought point five one are now in different classes, although they are nearly identical.

92
00:07:51,275 --> 00:07:58,550
And you threw it away in the output: the model can no longer tell you how helical something is, only which side it fell on.

93
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:00,800
So choose deliberately.

94
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:09,025
If the number matters to whoever will use your prediction, do not quietly turn it into a yes or no because yes or no is easier to score.

95
00:08:09,625 --> 00:08:14,350
Here are both kinds, on real data, so you can see what each looks like.

96
00:08:14,350 --> 00:08:16,625
On the left, a regression.

97
00:08:16,625 --> 00:08:19,600
Fifty-five entries from the PDB.

98
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:29,525
On the horizontal axis, the fraction of residues in that protein which are so-called helix formers, the amino acids that statistically prefer to be in helices.

99
00:08:29,525 --> 00:08:34,150
On the vertical axis, the actual helix fraction from the structure.

100
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:39,600
The points scatter around a rising line, with a correlation of nought point seven one.

101
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:44,150
Nought point seven one is a real relationship and not a strong one.

102
00:08:44,150 --> 00:08:52,375
You can see the scatter with your own eyes: for a given helix-former content, the actual helix fraction varies over quite a range.

103
00:08:52,375 --> 00:08:59,100
Keep that picture in mind, because in a few slides we will ask what nought point seven one is worth as a measure.

104
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:01,675
On the right, a classification.

105
00:09:01,675 --> 00:09:17,550
Four hundred and eighty-eight secreted proteins and four hundred and ninety-seven nuclear proteins, each plotted by two numbers: how hydrophobic its N-terminus is, and how much lysine and arginine it contains, those being the two positively charged residues.

106
00:09:17,550 --> 00:09:20,100
Look at the picture before the model.

107
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:25,300
The two classes separate almost by eye, into two blobs with some overlap.

108
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:34,200
The line drawn through them is logistic regression's decision boundary, placed where the estimated probability of being secreted is a half.

109
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:38,925
And that is a good habit in itself: plot the data before you model it.

110
00:09:38,925 --> 00:09:44,425
If the classes separate by eye in two dimensions, you do not need a deep network.

111
00:09:44,425 --> 00:09:50,800
If they do not separate at all, no model will rescue you, and you should go back and find better features.

112
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:59,800
Now a slide of history, because I want you to understand that the failure at the centre of this session is not a modern problem or a student problem.

113
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:04,050
It is as old as the field, and it happened to very good people.

114
00:10:04,050 --> 00:10:07,775
First, the biology, since the whole session leans on it.

115
00:10:07,775 --> 00:10:16,675
A signal peptide is a short stretch at the very beginning of a protein, typically fifteen to thirty residues, with a hydrophobic core in the middle.

116
00:10:16,675 --> 00:10:21,775
It is an address label: it says send this chain into the secretory pathway.

117
00:10:21,775 --> 00:10:28,875
In a eukaryotic cell that means into the endoplasmic reticulum; in bacteria, across the inner membrane.

118
00:10:28,875 --> 00:10:34,925
Once the protein has been pushed through, the signal peptide is cut off by an enzyme and discarded.

119
00:10:34,925 --> 00:10:37,875
You saw its motif in Session four.

120
00:10:37,875 --> 00:10:39,750
Now the history.

121
00:10:39,750 --> 00:10:41,950
Nineteen eighty-three.

122
00:10:41,950 --> 00:10:47,650
Somebody derives a rule for where the cleavage site is, from seventy-eight known sequences.

123
00:10:47,650 --> 00:10:52,450
On those seventy-eight sequences the rule is ninety-two per cent correct.

124
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:55,950
On new sequences, it is sixty-four per cent.

125
00:10:55,950 --> 00:10:58,725
Look at those two numbers on the slide.

126
00:10:58,725 --> 00:11:01,275
Ninety-two and sixty-four.

127
00:11:01,275 --> 00:11:02,875
Same rule.

128
00:11:02,875 --> 00:11:08,350
The gap is twenty-eight percentage points, and everything in Block two is about that gap.

129
00:11:08,350 --> 00:11:11,175
Nineteen eighty-five, McGeoch.

130
00:11:11,175 --> 00:11:17,750
Two features: the length of the uncharged stretch at the N-terminus, and the maximum hydrophobicity in it.

131
00:11:17,750 --> 00:11:23,200
The boundary between the classes was drawn by hand, by a person looking at a plot.

132
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:29,225
And it worked rather well, which is worth knowing when you are tempted to reach for something complicated.

133
00:11:29,225 --> 00:11:31,375
Nineteen ninety-one.

134
00:11:31,375 --> 00:11:37,650
A neural network that fitted every single training protein correctly, and then failed on new ones.

135
00:11:37,650 --> 00:11:42,725
That failure has a name, which we will use for the rest of the course: overfitting.

136
00:11:42,725 --> 00:11:47,175
The model did not learn the biology; it memorised the examples.

137
00:11:47,175 --> 00:12:00,125
And our own classifier, which we are about to look at, is deliberately the same two-feature idea as McGeoch's, except that we learn the boundary instead of drawing it by hand, and we test it on proteins it has never seen.

138
00:12:00,725 --> 00:12:04,900
So here is our version, and it is a satisfying result.

139
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:10,900
Logistic regression, two input features, trained on that secreted-versus-nuclear data.

140
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:18,125
Ninety-three point five per cent correct on held-out proteins, that is, proteins it did not see during training.

141
00:12:18,125 --> 00:12:22,550
Ninety-three and a half per cent, from two numbers per protein.

142
00:12:22,550 --> 00:12:25,975
No deep network, no alignment, no structure.

143
00:12:25,975 --> 00:12:30,150
Now look at the two weights the model learned, which are on the slide.

144
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:34,525
The weight on N-terminal hydrophobicity is four point two six.

145
00:12:34,525 --> 00:12:39,775
The weight on lysine-plus-arginine content is minus nought point two zero.

146
00:12:39,775 --> 00:12:43,425
Those two numbers are twenty times apart in size.

147
00:12:43,425 --> 00:12:46,650
The model is, in effect, using one feature.

148
00:12:46,650 --> 00:12:55,600
It has decided that N-terminal hydrophobicity is what matters, and that charge composition is almost irrelevant for this particular distinction.

149
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:58,500
And that is the biology, exactly.

150
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:02,575
The hydrophobic core of the signal peptide is the signal.

151
00:13:02,575 --> 00:13:13,550
Nobody told the model that signal peptides exist; it was given two numbers and a label, and it put nearly all its weight on the one that corresponds to a real targeting mechanism.

152
00:13:13,550 --> 00:13:20,250
This is a small example of something you will see repeatedly from here on: when a model works, look inside it.

153
00:13:20,250 --> 00:13:28,125
The weights often tell you which of your features was doing the work, and sometimes, as here, that is itself a biological result.

154
00:13:28,725 --> 00:13:33,975
This slide is how I would like you to report a classifier, so spend a moment on it.

155
00:13:33,975 --> 00:13:44,600
Rather than a single accuracy number, it shows every held-out protein individually, plotted by the probability the model assigned to it, and grouped by what it actually is.

156
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:48,575
The secreted proteins cluster up at high probability.

157
00:13:48,575 --> 00:13:52,525
The nuclear proteins cluster down at low probability.

158
00:13:52,525 --> 00:14:01,700
And the handful that sit on the wrong side of nought point five are exactly the model's errors, and you can see how many there are and how badly wrong they are.

159
00:14:01,700 --> 00:14:05,950
Two things this view gives you that an accuracy figure does not.

160
00:14:05,950 --> 00:14:08,050
First, confidence.

161
00:14:08,050 --> 00:14:18,125
A model that puts everything at nought point four nine or nought point five one would have the same accuracy and would be far less useful, because every call would be a coin flip.

162
00:14:18,125 --> 00:14:26,750
Here the two groups are pushed apart, which means the model is confident, and you can choose a stricter threshold if you want fewer false positives.

163
00:14:26,750 --> 00:14:31,475
Second, the errors are visible and countable, and you can go and look at them.

164
00:14:31,475 --> 00:14:35,100
In real work that is where the next idea comes from.

165
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:38,175
Are the mistakes all membrane proteins?

166
00:14:38,175 --> 00:14:40,450
All short sequences?

167
00:14:40,450 --> 00:14:45,400
A good afternoon's work is often just reading your model's twenty worst errors.

168
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Let me be precise about what logistic regression is, since we are using it as our baseline for the rest of the course.

169
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,575
Start with Session seven's perceptron: a weighted sum of the inputs, plus a bias.

170
00:14:58,575 --> 00:15:01,450
That gives you z, any real number.

171
00:15:01,450 --> 00:15:06,125
Then apply the sigmoid: one divided by one plus e to the minus z.

172
00:15:06,125 --> 00:15:12,725
That squashes z into the range between nought and one, smoothly, passing through a half when z is zero.

173
00:15:12,725 --> 00:15:15,425
That is logistic regression.

174
00:15:15,425 --> 00:15:20,250
It really is a perceptron with a smooth output instead of a hard step.

175
00:15:20,250 --> 00:15:22,675
Three things to keep straight.

176
00:15:22,675 --> 00:15:26,900
First, despite the name, it is a classifier, not a regression.

177
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:30,975
The name is historical and it confuses everyone once.

178
00:15:30,975 --> 00:15:34,675
Second, the decision boundary is still a straight line.

179
00:15:34,675 --> 00:15:45,250
The sigmoid changes the output from a verdict to a number between nought and one, but it does not bend the boundary: the boundary is still where z equals zero, which is still flat.

180
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:51,200
Everything Session seven said about what one weighted sum can and cannot represent still applies.

181
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:53,975
Third, and this is the subtle one.

182
00:15:53,975 --> 00:16:03,950
Because it is fitted with the right loss, which is the log-loss or cross-entropy, its output can be read as an estimate of the probability that this protein is secreted.

183
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:06,975
That is a genuinely useful property.

184
00:16:06,975 --> 00:16:10,625
But do not over-read it, and here is the distinction.

185
00:16:10,625 --> 00:16:15,350
The sigmoid is just a function that maps any number into zero to one.

186
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:23,975
If you apply a sigmoid to some arbitrary score you invented, the result is a number between nought and one and it is not a probability.

187
00:16:23,975 --> 00:16:31,825
Logistic regression's output is a probability estimate because of how it was fitted, not because of the shape of the sigmoid.

188
00:16:31,825 --> 00:16:43,175
And even then, whether the estimates are well calibrated, meaning that of the things it calls eighty per cent, about eighty per cent really are, is something you have to check on held-out data.

189
00:16:43,175 --> 00:16:45,450
It is not guaranteed.

190
00:16:46,050 --> 00:16:52,750
Now back to the regression-or-classification fork, because this slide is a reference card you will use all term.

191
00:16:52,750 --> 00:16:59,625
If you chose regression, your model's final layer outputs a plain number, with no activation squashing it.

192
00:16:59,625 --> 00:17:06,125
Your loss is the mean squared error, which is the squared-error loss of Session seven averaged over the data.

193
00:17:06,125 --> 00:17:12,500
And the metrics that mean something are the root mean squared error, the Pearson correlation, and R squared.

194
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:15,225
We will go through all three shortly.

195
00:17:15,225 --> 00:17:24,625
If you chose classification, your final layer outputs either a sigmoid, for two classes, or a softmax, for more than two, which we will meet in Block three.

196
00:17:24,625 --> 00:17:32,450
Your loss is cross-entropy, which you met in Session one as a measure of surprise and which we will use from now on without ceremony.

197
00:17:32,450 --> 00:17:45,350
And the metrics are a longer list, because classification goes wrong in more interesting ways: the confusion matrix, precision, recall, F1, the Matthews correlation coefficient, and the area under the ROC curve.

198
00:17:45,350 --> 00:17:48,425
Everything on that list is in Block two.

199
00:17:48,425 --> 00:17:55,225
The point of showing it now is that you should see how much follows automatically from one decision made at the start.

200
00:17:55,825 --> 00:18:01,000
For Block two we need a problem small enough that nothing is hidden, and this is it.

201
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,075
Nine pixels, arranged three by three.

202
00:18:04,075 --> 00:18:11,150
Five classes, each a simple pattern: a horizontal bar, a vertical bar, a diagonal, a cross, and so on.

203
00:18:11,150 --> 00:18:15,600
Dark pixels count as plus one and light pixels as minus one.

204
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:17,650
Then we add noise.

205
00:18:17,650 --> 00:18:27,075
Each pixel is jittered by a random amount with a standard deviation of nought point eight, which is substantial compared with the signal of plus or minus one.

206
00:18:27,075 --> 00:18:33,200
So the patterns are recognisable but genuinely corrupted, and the problem is not trivially easy.

207
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:35,775
Why go to the trouble of a toy?

208
00:18:35,775 --> 00:18:44,700
Because every number today can be computed, printed and checked by hand, and because the confusion structure is known in advance rather than discovered.

209
00:18:44,700 --> 00:18:47,625
And that last point is the useful one.

210
00:18:47,625 --> 00:18:49,450
Look at the cross.

211
00:18:49,450 --> 00:18:55,700
The cross shares its middle row with the horizontal bar, and its middle column with the vertical bar.

212
00:18:55,700 --> 00:18:59,275
It overlaps two other classes by construction.

213
00:18:59,275 --> 00:19:06,875
So before training anything, we can predict where the mistakes will be: between cross and horizontal, and between cross and vertical.

214
00:19:06,875 --> 00:19:14,850
When we look at a confusion matrix in a few slides and those are exactly the cells that light up, you will know you are reading it correctly.

215
00:19:14,850 --> 00:19:17,500
That is why we start with a toy.

216
00:19:17,500 --> 00:19:19,200
One aside.

217
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:25,975
Image classification is where deep learning first proved itself, and we come to that properly in Session ten.

218
00:19:25,975 --> 00:19:31,475
Nine pixels is a long way from a photograph, but it is the same shape of problem.

219
00:19:32,075 --> 00:19:38,775
And the model for it is deliberately the simplest thing that works, so that nothing in Block two is about the model.

220
00:19:38,775 --> 00:19:41,650
Five perceptrons, one per class.

221
00:19:41,650 --> 00:19:47,850
Each one is trained to output plus one for its own pattern and minus one for all four of the others.

222
00:19:47,850 --> 00:19:52,675
That arrangement has a name you will meet in libraries: one-versus-rest.

223
00:19:52,675 --> 00:19:59,100
To classify a new image, you run all five, and the class whose perceptron gave the highest score wins.

224
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:01,525
Notice what this is not.

225
00:20:01,525 --> 00:20:08,100
There is no hidden layer, so there is no backpropagation involved and nothing from Session nine is needed.

226
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:14,300
Each perceptron is Session seven's single weighted sum, trained with Session seven's machinery.

227
00:20:14,300 --> 00:20:23,625
And notice the limitation we are accepting on purpose: with no hidden layer, each of the five is drawing one flat boundary in nine-dimensional space.

228
00:20:23,625 --> 00:20:28,075
We know from Session seven that there are patterns it cannot capture.

229
00:20:28,075 --> 00:20:29,850
That is fine.

230
00:20:29,850 --> 00:20:32,800
The point of this model is not to be good.

231
00:20:32,800 --> 00:20:39,050
It is to be completely transparent, so that when we evaluate it, we are evaluating the evaluation.

